[1mCAVEATS[0m
       Failure  to observe the requirement that the memory areas do not overlap has been
       the source of significant bugs.  (POSIX and the C standards are explicit that em‐
       ploying [1mmemcpy[22m() with overlapping areas produces undefined behavior.)   Most  no‐
       tably,  in  glibc  2.13  a performance optimization of [1mmemcpy[22m() on some platforms
       (including x86-64) included changing the order in which bytes  were  copied  from
       [4msrc[24m to [4mdest[24m.

       This change revealed breakages in a number of applications that performed copying
       with  overlapping  areas.   Under the previous implementation, the order in which
       the bytes were copied had fortuitously hidden the bug, which  was  revealed  when
       the  copying  order was reversed.  In glibc 2.14, a versioned symbol was added so
       that old binaries (i.e., those linked against glibc versions earlier  than  2.14)
       employed  a  [1mmemcpy[22m()  implementation that safely handles the overlapping buffers
       case (by providing an "older" [1mmemcpy[22m() implementation that was  aliased  to  [1mmem‐[0m
       [1mmove[22m(3)).

